# vcf-ops-mcp, slot .env contract.
#
# Copy this file to /srv/services/vcf-ops-mcp/.env on the docker.int slot and
# fill in the placeholder with a real generated value.
#
# THIS FILE IS NEVER WRITTEN BY CI. The deploy workflow writes only
# images.env, which it owns and which carries the pinned image digest. The
# .env below is owned by the host owner (lab-admin) and survives every deploy.
# The two are passed to Compose as separate --env-file arguments, in that
# order. Do not merge them, and do not let a deploy scp over .env.
#
# No real secret value ever appears in this repository, in CI logs, or in a
# commit. This file documents variable names and formats only.
#
# --- Required ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# SESSION_SECRET signs the admin UI session cookie. The server refuses to
# build its application at all when it is unset (create_app raises), and the
# compose file's ${SESSION_SECRET:?...} form makes a missing value stop the
# deploy with a named error instead of starting a crash-looping container.
#
# Format: a long, random, single-line string. No quotes, no spaces, no
# trailing newline weirdness. At least 32 bytes of entropy.
#
# Generate once per slot:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Rotating it invalidates every outstanding admin session at once, which is
# the intended blast radius: there is no second accepted key and no grace
# window, so rotation means every admin signs in again.
SESSION_SECRET=<generate a real secret at the slot, never commit it>

# --- Optional ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# AUDIT_DB_PATH overrides where the durable audit ledger lives. The default,
# /audit/audit.sqlite3, is on the audit_log volume and is correct for this
# slot. Setting this to a path outside a volume would put the audit log on the
# read-only root filesystem, so leave it unset unless the volume layout
# changes.
#AUDIT_DB_PATH=/audit/audit.sqlite3

# LOG_LEVEL sets the root Python log level. Defaults to INFO.
#LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# --- Not in this file at all ------------------------------------------------
#
# VCF Ops target FQDNs, their credentials, the API keys clients present, and
# the audit ledger itself are runtime data, not deploy configuration. They are
# entered through the admin UI and held in the encrypted-at-rest store on the
# credential_store volume and in the ledger on the audit_log volume. Those
# volumes are the only part of this deployment that cannot be rebuilt from
# git. Back them up; do not `docker compose down -v` them.
